00:00.000 --> 00:05.440
So, if we were to naively make use of the Löfverberg-Macquardt algorithm, we will find

00:05.440 --> 00:12.440
ourselves quickly running into a cubic complexity at every iteration, since we are going to

00:12.440 --> 00:20.760
solve for the normal equation given by J transpose J delta equals to minus J transpose epsilon

00:20.760 --> 00:26.640
over here. And this, we have seen that this is equivalent to an inhomogeneous linear equation

00:26.640 --> 00:35.000
of A X equals to zero. And we all know that when we solve for this inhomogeneous linear

00:35.000 --> 00:40.960
equation for X, which is equivalent to delta over here, we have to invert this guy over

00:40.960 --> 00:49.360
here, the A matrix, in order to solve for A, which is equal to inverse of A multiplied

00:49.360 --> 00:58.040
by B. And this incurs a cubic complexity where n over here can be very large. And this is

00:58.040 --> 01:03.880
actually in the order of the total number of camera parameters over all camera, so it

01:03.880 --> 01:10.000
would be 12 multiplied by the total number of camera in our 3D reconstruction, as well

01:10.000 --> 01:15.080
as the total number of 3D points, that would be three coordinates of X, Y, Z multiplied

01:15.080 --> 01:24.160
by the total number of 3D points in the scene. In a 3D reconstruction problem, this can easily

01:24.160 --> 01:34.080
be up to an order of millions of 3D points. So fortunately, we will exploit the sparse

01:34.080 --> 01:40.840
block structure in this A matrix, which is equivalent to the J transpose J matrix over

01:40.840 --> 01:49.560
here, in order to mitigate the computational complexity. More specifically, we first divide

01:49.560 --> 01:57.800
or partition the set of parameters P, which is an m-dimensional parameter, into two sets

01:57.800 --> 02:03.880
of parameter vectors which we call A and B. So what this means is that I'm going to rewrite

02:03.880 --> 02:14.480
P into P1, P2, all the way to Pm here, and I'm going to partition this set of parameters

02:14.480 --> 02:19.760
into two sets, where I call the first set of parameters over here a vector of A, and

02:19.760 --> 02:26.320
the second set would be a vector of B over here, and I'm going to rewrite this P as A

02:26.320 --> 02:33.960
transpose, B transpose, and altogether transpose. So this, in the context of our bandai adjustment

02:33.960 --> 02:42.680
problem, A could represent all our camera parameters, and B here could represent all

02:42.680 --> 02:48.880
the parameters from the 3D points, so we can partition them into two sets of parameters

02:48.880 --> 02:56.520
