column over here. And what this means is that I'm going to reorder the permutation matrix given

32:52.980 --> 32:59.060
square matrix over here. All the entries in this particular matrix would be reordered,

32:59.060 --> 33:05.940
that means that I will have to sort some of the entries by simply applying the permutation matrix

33:05.940 --> 33:12.740
on the A matrix. I'll not go into the detail of this, I will just simply give you a flavour of

33:14.100 --> 33:20.980
how to do the reordering or what does the reordering mean over here. So unfortunately,

33:20.980 --> 33:26.740
this particular problem to reorder the sparse matrix is an NP-complete problem,

33:26.740 --> 33:35.940
and this means that it could become intractable to compute for the best permutation, such that

33:35.940 --> 33:44.420
we reorder the A matrix to minimise the amount of fill-in after, for example, Kolosky decomposition.

33:44.420 --> 33:50.580
There are some approximation algorithms such as the minimum degree, the column approximate minimum

33:51.220 --> 33:57.060
permutation, reverse, McCutcheon-Mckee algorithm and the nested dissection algorithm.

33:57.060 --> 34:01.780
I will not go into the detail of this algorithm, if you are interested you should read the

